# ratings_elo.json is deliberately tracked, not ignored: it was seeded
# 2026-07-26 with a one-time historical backfill (see backfill_history.py
# and PROVIDER_COMPLIANCE.md) so the live site starts from real historical
# Elo instead of a blank slate. The GitHub Actions cache (a separate system
# from git, see deploy.yml's actions/cache step) is the normal place this
# file lives day-to-day; this is a one-time seed exception, not a new
# standing convention -- don't add other generated files here on this
# precedent without the same explicit reasoning.
ratings_h2h.json
# picks_log*.json is deliberately tracked too (2026-07-27), for the same
# reason ratings*.json is: it's the pick-lock ledger the entire scorecard's
# "Locked in public, never rewritten" claim depends on, and living only in
# the GitHub Actions cache meant a single cache-restore hiccup could reset
# it to empty with zero trace -- confirmed live the same day this comment
# was added. Git is now the source of truth; deploy.yml commits it back
# after every fetch, the same way it already does for ratings.
.browser-profile/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.ci_fetch_state.json
provider_quota_state.json
.x_bot_state.json
# posts.json, posts_state.json and posts/*.html were listed here as
# "CI-owned, cache-only" while being tracked in git anyway -- ignoring a
# tracked file changes nothing, so checkout kept writing them and the cache
# step kept restoring an older copy straight over the top. That is the same
# restore-keys clobber documented above for ratings and picks_log, and it
# had the same result: a posts.json culled by the comp-filter bug (fixed in
# generate_posts.py) was cached, then restored over every corrected
# checkout, so the fix could not take and the hourly deploy stayed red.
# Git is now the source of truth for the published record; deploy.yml
# commits these back after every fetch and no longer caches them. The post
# HTML has to travel with posts.json or the site links entries whose pages
# 404 (friday-model-vs-market-2026-08-07 and
# wednesday-availability-tracker-2026-08-05 were in that state, reachable
# only for as long as the cache happened to hold them).
#
# research_posts.json and posts/research-*.html stay untracked: unlike the
# editorial posts they really are rebuilt deterministically by
# build_research_posts.py from docs/experiments.json + picks_log_*.json on
# every regenerate_sitemap() call, so losing them costs nothing.
research_posts.json
posts/research-*.html
_site/
node_modules/
# forecast_ledger_*.jsonl and mlb_shadow_ledger.jsonl were cache-only until
# 2026-08-18, sitting on the exact mechanism that had already silently reset
# picks_log once: the cache step's restore-keys fallback can hand a run an
# older copy (or nothing at all on a cold miss) with no error trace. These
# append-only ledgers ARE the prospective evidence behind every "locked
# pregame, never rewritten" claim and behind the NFL/MLB promotion gates --
# losing one silently discards months of receipts that cannot be
# reconstructed after the fact, because the whole point is that they were
# written before kickoff. They now get the same durable-source-of-truth
# treatment as ratings*.json and picks_log*.json: committed back to git after
# every fetch (see "Commit updated ratings and picks ledger" in deploy.yml),
# with git as the one source of truth and the cache as pure accelerator.
advanced_metrics_*.json
nflverse_pbp_*.csv.gz
nfl_challenger_training_rows.jsonl
nfl_prospective_scorecard.json
mlb_prospective_scorecard.json
nfl_availability_ledger.jsonl
